<main class="wrap">

  <p class="status">Preprint · Version 2 · Under review · November 2026</p>
  <h1>Consent as Infrastructure</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Keeping AI agents tied to the people they act for</p>
  <p class="byline">Preethika Reddy Mandadi · Medifakt, Hyderabad, India</p>

  <div class="buttons">
    <a class="btn primary" href="Mandadi_Consent_as_Infrastructure_preprint_v2.pdf">Download PDF</a>
    <a class="btn secondary" href="#cite">Cite this paper</a>
  </div>

  <section id="abstract">
    <h2>Abstract</h2>
    <p class="kicker">Authorization should not end at the grant</p>
    <p>AI agents increasingly act on delegated authority. They read untrusted content, call tools, spawn sub-agents, and take consequential actions on a person's behalf. Yet authorization for these agents is usually granted once and checked against a fixed grant, while the record of what was done is held by the operator. This paper argues that this arrangement quietly shifts control away from the person whose authority is being exercised, and that delegated authority should instead remain tied to that person throughout execution.</p>
    <p>Drawing on healthcare's experience in separating the recording of consent from its enforcement, the paper identifies three gaps that the two closest agent-authorization specifications leave open, derives six requirements for a principal-controlled authorization lifecycle, and separates what existing mechanisms already provide from what the requirements add. It frames those requirements as conditions for preserving the principal's autonomy: the ability to bound, revise, understand, and contest what an agent does in their name.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="claims">
    <h2>Three claims</h2>
    <p class="kicker">What current specifications leave open</p>
    <ol class="claims">
      <li><strong>Purpose is a runtime constraint, not an audit label.</strong> A task's declared purpose should limit what the agent may do while it runs, checked at the enforcement point, with escalation to the principal where it cannot be decided deterministically.</li>
      <li><strong>The principal can change scope mid-task.</strong> The person should be able to narrow, widen, or withdraw a class of actions while the agent runs. That differs from approving one held action at a time, and from automatic attenuation triggered by risk signals.</li>
      <li><strong>The principal can read the record.</strong> An intelligible view of what the agent attempted, what was allowed or blocked, under which grant, and where the triggering instruction came from belongs to the person whose authority was used, not only to the operator.</li>
    </ol>

    <figure>
      <img src="figure1.png" alt="Diagram: the principal grants, changes, or revokes an authorization record; the record sets scope and purpose for an enforcement point; agents and sub-agents propose actions to the enforcement point; the enforcement point passes only allowed actions to tools and services; every decision is logged to an audit record that the principal can read.">
      <figcaption>Figure 1. A principal-controlled authorization lifecycle. Every agent action is checked against a record over which the principal holds authority.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="proposal">
    <h2>The proposal</h2>
    <p class="kicker">Six requirements for a principal-controlled lifecycle</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Record delegation; never infer it.</li>
      <li>Enforce independently of the agent, so every consequential action passes one gate.</li>
      <li>Enforce purpose at runtime.</li>
      <li>Let the principal change scope during execution.</li>
      <li>Narrow and trace delegation chains across sub-agents.</li>
      <li>Make the record readable by the principal.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The paper works these through a travel-booking example, sets out a threat model and open design questions, and proposes four additions that current drafts (AIMS-01 in the IETF WIMSE working group and the AAE individual draft) could adopt without new cryptography or a new protocol. It also states its limits: it does not solve alignment, and it depends on complete mediation.</p>
  </section>

